    {
      "content": "For a fixed integer n > 1, define F_n(x) = floor(n x) - sum_{k=1}^{n} floor(k x)/k for real x.",
      "kind": "definition",
      "label": "F_n"
    },
    {
      "content": "H_n = sum_{k=1}^{n} 1/k, the nth harmonic number.",
      "kind": "notation",
      "label": "H_n"
    },
    {
      "content": "frac(x) = x - floor(x), so frac(x) lies in [0,1).",
      "kind": "notation",
      "label": "frac"
    }
  ],
  "strategy_summary": "Reduce the problem modulo 1, then use the partition of [0,1) into the intervals [m/n,(m+1)/n). On each interval the function is nonincreasing, so the maximum there occurs at the left endpoint m/n. The only hard step is a discrete floor-sum inequality at those rational endpoints, which shows the unique global maximizers come from the last interval [ (n-1)/n, 1 ) modulo integers.",
